course: geography
lesson: 51
title: Gemstones and precious metals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A gemstone is a stone people cut and polish because it is beautiful, rare and hard enough to last. Most gems are minerals that grew as crystals deep in the Earth.
explain: Think of a grandmother's ring with a sparkling diamond. That diamond is pure carbon, the same stuff as pencil "lead" and soot, but squeezed so hard about 150 km down in the mantle that its atoms locked into a very hard crystal.
explain: Diamonds reach us only because rare volcanic pipes once shot them up to the surface fast. Rubies and sapphires are one mineral, corundum, and a pinch of another element decides the colour.
explain: Gold, silver and platinum are precious metals. Gold never rusts, so a gold coin from a shipwreck still shines. But the story of gems and metals also has a dark side: slaves, forced miners and wars paid for with stones.
fact: A gemstone is a mineral, or sometimes a material like amber, that is beautiful, rare and hard enough to cut and polish.
fact: Diamond is pure carbon squeezed deep in the mantle, about 150 km down, and carried up by rare volcanic pipes.
fact: Until the 1700s almost all the world's diamonds came from India, from the Golconda region.
fact: Rubies and sapphires are the same mineral, corundum; red corundum is ruby, and other colours are sapphire.
fact: Tiny traces of other elements, such as chromium in ruby, give corundum its colour.
fact: Emeralds are green beryl, and the finest came from Colombia, where the Spanish took them from the 1500s.
fact: Gold does not rust or tarnish and is soft enough to shape, so it has been treasured for thousands of years.
fact: In the 1300s much of the gold used in Europe and North Africa came from West Africa, traded across the Sahara through the empire of Mali, whose king Mansa Musa became famous for his huge wealth in gold.
fact: The Spanish forced Indigenous people to mine the silver mountain of Potosí in Bolivia, and many thousands died there.
fact: Platinum is rarer than gold and is used in car parts called catalytic converters that clean exhaust fumes.
fact: Some gems are not minerals: amber is fossil tree resin, sometimes with insects trapped inside, and pearls are made by oysters and mussels.
fact: Diamonds and other gems have paid for wars, so "conflict diamonds" are now meant to be tracked to show where they came from.
exercise: What makes a stone a gemstone? | It is beautiful, rare and hard enough to cut and polish | It is any stone that is heavy for its size and dark in colour | It is any stone found deep in a mine | It is any stone that glows in the dark
exercise: What is a diamond made of? | Pure carbon | Pure silicon | Hardened glass | Frozen salt water
exercise: Where do diamonds form? | Deep in the mantle, about 150 km down | In the hot sand of deserts, baked by the sun | Inside the shells of sea creatures | Just under the ground in meadows
exercise: How do diamonds get up to where people can mine them? | Rare volcanic pipes carried them up | Rivers washed them down from the clouds | Earthworms pushed them up slowly | They grew upwards like plant roots
exercise: Before the 1700s, where did almost all the world's diamonds come from? | India, from the Golconda region | South Africa, from the Kimberley mines | Russia, from frozen Siberia | Australia, from the outback
exercise: What mineral are rubies and sapphires? | Both are corundum | Both are clear quartz | Both are diamond | Both are green beryl
exercise: Why can corundum be red in one stone and blue in another? | Tiny traces of other elements colour it | Red ones are much hotter inside than blue ones | Blue ones have been soaked in the sea | Red ones are older than blue ones
exercise: What is an emerald? | Green beryl | Green bottle glass | Green diamond | Green corundum
exercise: Which country gave the finest emeralds, taken by the Spanish from the 1500s? | Colombia | Portugal | Egypt | Japan
exercise: Why has gold been treasured for thousands of years? | It does not rust and is soft enough to shape | It is the hardest metal of all, harder than steel | It is the most common metal in rocks | It glows with its own light
exercise: In the 1300s, where did much of Europe's and North Africa's gold come from? | West Africa, traded across the Sahara through Mali | China, carried west along the Silk Road by camels | The Americas, by Spanish ships | The Alps, from Swiss mines
exercise: What happened at the silver mountain of Potosí in Bolivia? | The Spanish forced Indigenous people to mine it and thousands died | Free miners grew rich there and retired young to big houses in Spain | It was mined only by machines from the start | It was kept secret and never mined at all
exercise: Which metal is rarer than gold and helps clean car exhaust fumes? | Platinum | Red copper | Cast iron | Tin
exercise: Which of these gems is not a mineral? | Amber, which is fossil tree resin | Ruby, which is red corundum | Emerald, which is green beryl | Diamond, which is pure carbon, a crystal
exercise: Where do pearls come from? | They are made by oysters and mussels | They grow in volcanic pipes deep down | They form slowly from drips in caves | They are dug from coal seams
exercise: What are "conflict diamonds"? | Diamonds sold to pay for wars | Diamonds with cracks inside them | Diamonds that two jewellers argue over | Diamonds made in a factory
test: Jake reckons diamonds and pencil lead are totally different stuff. right? | No, both are carbon, just arranged differently | Yes, diamond is made of crushed glass pressed hard | Yes, pencil lead is real lead metal | Yes, diamond is a frozen liquid
test: lol my cousin says sapphires r just blue rubies basically. is she wrong | She's about right: both are corundum, ruby is the red kind | Totally wrong, sapphire is a kind of diamond, ruby isn't | Totally wrong, sapphire is coloured glass, not a gem | Totally wrong, they're nothing alike
test: A cartoon shows a pirate digging up diamonds on a sandy beach next to a palm tree, "where diamonds grow". What should be corrected? | Diamonds form about 150 km down and come up in volcanic pipes | Diamonds only form inside ice in the Arctic | Diamonds grow on trees, not in sand | Diamonds form in the clouds and fall as hail
test: An old gold coin and an old iron nail are pulled from a shipwreck after 300 years. What will they probably look like? | The coin still shines, the nail is badly rusted | Both are badly rusted and fall apart in your hand | The coin is rusted, the nail shines | Both shine like new
test: Mansa Musa, a famous king of Mali in the 1300s, was known for huge amounts of what? | Gold | Diamonds from India | Silver from Bolivia | Platinum from Russia
test: Mia's necklace has a fly stuck inside a golden-brown stone. What is the stone? | Amber, fossil tree resin | Topaz, a hard yellow crystal | Ruby, a red kind of corundum | Quartz, a very common mineral
test: "shut up, pearls come out of rocks like every other gem" — what is the truth? | Pearls are made by oysters and mussels, not rocks | Pearls form in volcanic pipes like diamonds do | Pearls are tiny diamonds | Pearls are drops of melted silver that cooled
test: What would happen if no one checked where diamonds came from? | Diamonds mined to pay for wars could be sold freely | Diamonds would stop being hard | Diamonds would slowly turn back into black carbon dust | Diamonds would all become cheap glass
test: A game shows Potosí as a happy mining town where everyone got rich. What is the true story? | Indigenous people were forced to mine it and many thousands died | It was a quiet farming village with no mine and no silver at all | It was mined by robots in the 1500s | Its miners were all paid well and free
test: Why is a car's exhaust part one of the places a thief might steal precious metal from? | It contains platinum, a metal rarer than gold | It is made of solid gold under the paint | It is full of emeralds | It holds a tank of liquid silver
test: Which pair is made of the same mineral? | A ruby and a sapphire | A ruby and a green emerald | A diamond and a sea pearl | An emerald and a piece of amber
exam: Identify the principal reason for which gold has been esteemed across several millennia. | It is resistant to corrosion and sufficiently malleable to be worked | It is the hardest substance hitherto discovered by mineralogists | It is the most abundant metal in the Earth's crust | It emits light of its own accord
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of diamonds. | They crystallise from carbon at great depth in the mantle and ascend through volcanic pipes | They are deposited by rivers upon the surface of deserts | They are secreted by marine molluscs | They precipitate from rainwater within caverns
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises rubies and sapphires? | Both are varieties of corundum, distinguished by trace elements | Both are varieties of beryl, distinguished by their age and depth | Rubies are organic and sapphires mineral | Sapphires are artificial and rubies natural
exam: Pursuant to the tracking of so-termed "conflict diamonds", identify the purpose of such tracking. | To demonstrate that gems have not financed armed conflict | To certify the hardness of each gem | To record the weight of each gem for taxation only | To ascertain the colour of each gem
